It is the middle of the night, and this is your nursery, the top room of a tall house in London, where you and your two brothers have slept every night of your lives. You are Wendy Darling, the eldest, and just now you are the only one awake. Across the room, John and Michael sleep on. Your mother and father are still out at their dinner party, so the house is still, and not quite right. The night-light has burned out. The window your mother always latches stands wide open to the cold.

And there is a boy on the floor.

He is no older than you, dressed all in skeleton leaves, and he is crying the way a child cries over a toy that has broken. The broken thing is his own shadow, come loose of him and refusing to go back on. He has been trying to fasten it with a cake of soap, which is, of course, no way to mend a shadow at all.

His name, he tells you, is Peter Pan. And perhaps you did know it, for you have heard of him in stories. He has flown in from a place called Neverland, where there are boys with no mothers, and no one to tuck them in or tell them tales at night. That, he says, is what he came for. Someone to give the lost boys their stories. Someone to be their mother.

Then he holds out his hand, bare and certain, and asks you to come with him, out the open window, off into the dark, this very night.
